1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,160
Patulous Eustachian Tube, could a diuretic or your birth control pill be behind that strange echo in your ear?

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:16,000
If your own voice or breathing sounds oddly loud inside your ear, the cause isn't always what you'd expect.

3
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:24,800
The Eustachian tube, the passage connecting your middle ear to the back of your nose, is supposed to stay closed most of the time.

4
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:34,880
When it stays open instead, sound travels straight through to your eardrum, so your own voice echoes and your ear can feel full or clogged.

5
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,760
Weight loss is the risk factor doctors mention most often for this condition.

6
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:48,160
A fat pad around the Eustachian tube normally helps hold it closed, and losing weight quickly thins that padding out.

7
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:54,800
Among patients after bariatric surgery, roughly one in five report symptoms that match it.

8
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,520
There's a second cause that's less talked about: certain medications.

9
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:12,560
Diuretics and estrogen-containing birth control pills have both been linked to a patulous Eustachian tube, since they change fluid balance in a way that can thin the tissue lining the tube.

10
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:19,760
If your symptoms started around the same time as a new prescription, that timing is worth mentioning to your doctor.

11
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:28,560
Stress and exercise tend to make symptoms worse, while lying down or bending forward often brings quick relief.

12
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:37,280
That's because blood flow shifts toward your head in that position, swelling the tissue around the tube just enough to narrow it again.

13
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:43,680
If you're on a diuretic or birth control pill, ask your doctor whether an alternative makes sense.

14
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Saline nasal spray used with your head tilted down is worth trying for quick relief, and it's worth booking a visit with an ENT doctor if symptoms keep affecting your daily life.
